"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdocs_site=self.webpackChunkdocs_site||[]).push([["5399"],{8851(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/10/18/case-studies","metadata":{"permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/case-studies","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-case-studies.md","title":"Case Study: Vishap on curl (cookie.c + http2.c)","description":"Two production files from curl, nine findings, five CWE classes.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"case-study","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/case-study"},{"inline":true,"label":"security","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":1.92,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Case Study: Vishap on curl (cookie.c + http2.c)","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["case-study","security"],"description":"Two production files from curl, nine findings, five CWE classes."},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Vishap v6.3.0 \u2014 Next.js Dashboard","permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/v630"}},"content":"We pointed Vishap at two files from curl: `lib/cookie.c` and `lib/http2.c`.\\nBetween them it reported nine findings across five CWE classes. None is a proven\\nvulnerability \u2014 these are triage candidates \u2014 but the shape of the results is\\nworth writing down.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## lib/cookie.c \u2014 one critical\\n\\nThe critical finding is a data race (`CWE-362`): `remove_expired` and\\n`storecookie` mutate the shared cookie list without synchronisation visible in\\nthe file. Whether that is exploitable depends on a share-lock contract defined\\nelsewhere \u2014 which is exactly why the report says `REQUIRES_REVIEW` rather than\\nasserting a vulnerability.\\n\\nThree further findings: a NUL byte ends validation early in `invalid_octets`\\n(`CWE-20`), an out-of-bounds read when indexing the cookie pieces array\\n(`CWE-125`), and a partially initialised cookie left behind on allocation\\nfailure (`CWE-459`).\\n\\n## lib/http2.c \u2014 error paths\\n\\nThe HTTP/2 file produced five findings, all on error paths; the happy path\\nlooked correct. Return values of stream initialisers go unchecked (`CWE-252`),\\n`cf_h2_ctx_free` assumes a partially initialised context (`CWE-459`), a reachable\\n`DEBUGASSERT(0)` compiles out to a silent `NULL` in release builds (`CWE-617`),\\nand two optional items: window-size desync after a failed submit (`CWE-754`) and\\nan unwritten single-thread contract (`CWE-362`).\\n\\n## What the reports looked like\\n\\nEach analysis produced the same structure: findings with CWE and severity, a\\nSTRIDE model tied to those findings, three refactoring options with effort\\nestimates, and an audit trail with both the analysed-slice hash and the original\\nmaterial hash.\\n\\nFor `cookie.c` the recommended option was contract-hardening: fix the three\\nlocal defects in two to three days and leave the locking architecture alone\\nuntil someone proves concurrent access matters. For `http2.c` the same \u2014 the\\nthree important findings are all \\"propagate the failure\\" changes, not redesigns.\\n\\nRead the full case studies:\\n[cookie.c](/docs/case-studies/curl-cookie-c) and\\n[http2.c](/docs/case-studies/curl-http2-c).\\n\\n## The honest caveats\\n\\n- No proof of concept was built; nothing was fuzzed.\\n- The material was truncated, which limits confidence in control flow across the\\n  cut.\\n- Heuristic LLM analysis produces false positives. Some of these nine may be\\n  wrong, and some may already be known to curl\'s maintainers.\\n\\nWe drafted a disclosure note to `security@curl.se` describing both files. It is\\nnot sent yet."},{"id":"/2026/10/18/v630","metadata":{"permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/v630","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-v630.md","title":"Vishap v6.3.0 \u2014 Next.js Dashboard","description":"A Next.js dashboard, OpenTelemetry tracing, and multi-tenant isolation by default.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"release","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"ui","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/ui"},{"inline":true,"label":"observability","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/observability"}],"readingTime":1.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Vishap v6.3.0 \u2014 Next.js Dashboard","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["release","ui","observability"],"description":"A Next.js dashboard, OpenTelemetry tracing, and multi-tenant isolation by default."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Case Study: Vishap on curl (cookie.c + http2.c)","permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/case-studies"},"nextItem":{"title":"Vishap v6.3.1 \u2014 Multi-tenant RLS + Audit Export","permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/v631"}},"content":"v6.3.0 replaces the Streamlit prototype with a Next.js dashboard, adds\\nOpenTelemetry tracing across the whole pipeline, and turns multi-tenant\\nisolation into something the database enforces rather than something each query\\nis expected to remember.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## A real UI\\n\\nThe new dashboard is Next.js 16 with Clerk authentication. It does three things\\nthe prototype could not: it shows a list of jobs with live status, it renders\\nreports as formatted Markdown instead of a text blob, and it keeps a per-browser\\njob history you can return to.\\n\\nThe analyse form takes a file or pasted material, submits the job, polls it, and\\nredirects to the report when the pipeline finishes. Nothing else to learn.\\n\\n## Tracing you can follow\\n\\nEvery analysis produces an OpenTelemetry trace: one span per pipeline stage, one\\nfor the Celery task, exported over OTLP to Tempo and readable in Grafana. When a\\njob takes 40 seconds instead of 12, you can see which stage spent the time rather\\nthan guessing.\\n\\nThe instrumentation is fail-open. If OpenTelemetry is not installed or\\n`OTEL_SDK_DISABLED` is set, the application runs exactly as before and the spans\\nbecome no-ops. Tracing never becomes a startup dependency.\\n\\n## Multi-tenant isolation in the database\\n\\nJobs are now isolated with PostgreSQL row-level security. Each request binds a\\ntenant, and a policy restricts `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` to rows\\nbelonging to that tenant \u2014 including the table owner, once\\n`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` is enabled. The application also sets the tenant on\\nevery session it opens, so a query that forgets to filter is still filtered.\\n\\nThis matters more than it sounds. Isolation that lives only in application code\\nis one forgotten `WHERE` away from a cross-tenant leak. Isolation that lives in\\na policy is not.\\n\\n## The basics\\n\\n- The job store is fully async, which fixed a real bug: the PostgreSQL backend\'s\\n  coroutines were never awaited, so async update paths silently did nothing.\\n- The analysis history is tenant-scoped, including legacy rows with no tenant,\\n  which belong to `public`.\\n- Audit-trail export is available as CSV or JSON for compliance collection.\\n\\nUpgrade notes are in the [documentation](/docs/getting-started)."},{"id":"/2026/10/18/v631","metadata":{"permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/v631","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-v631.md","title":"Vishap v6.3.1 \u2014 Multi-tenant RLS + Audit Export","description":"Row-level security, audit export, GDPR endpoints, and two curl case studies.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"release","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"security","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/security"},{"inline":true,"label":"compliance","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/compliance"}],"readingTime":2.19,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Vishap v6.3.1 \u2014 Multi-tenant RLS + Audit Export","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["release","security","compliance"],"description":"Row-level security, audit export, GDPR endpoints, and two curl case studies."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Vishap v6.3.0 \u2014 Next.js Dashboard","permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/v630"},"nextItem":{"title":"Welcome to Vishap","permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/welcome"}},"content":"v6.3.1 is the release where isolation moved from application code into the\\ndatabase, and where the audit trail became something you can hand to an auditor\\ninstead of something you read in a dashboard.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Row-level security\\n\\nJobs are now isolated by a PostgreSQL policy rather than by remembering to add a\\n`WHERE tenant_id = ...` to every query. Every request binds a tenant, and the\\npolicy restricts `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` to rows belonging to\\nit. `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` extends that to the table owner, so a future\\nmigration script cannot quietly bypass it.\\n\\nThe application also sets the tenant on each session through\\n`set_config(\'app.current_tenant\', ..., true)`, which means a query that forgets\\nto filter is still filtered by the database. Two independent layers, one\\nmistake required in each.\\n\\nThis release also fixed a real bug found while building it: the PostgreSQL job\\nstore is async, and its coroutines were never awaited. Async update paths\\nreturned a pending coroutine and silently did nothing. The store interface is\\nnow fully async end to end.\\n\\n## Audit export\\n\\n`GET /audit/export?format=csv` and `?format=json` return the caller\'s job trail\\nwith filters for date range, status and limit. The CSV streams row by row; the\\nJSON mirrors the same eight columns. Report bodies are deliberately excluded \u2014\\nan auditor wants to know who did what and when, not the contents of a report.\\n\\nThis is the artifact SOC 2 and ISO 27001 evidence collection actually asks for.\\n\\n## GDPR endpoints\\n\\n`GET /api/me/data` exports every job and history entry for the tenant;\\n`DELETE /api/me` erases them. Both are scoped by tenant, and both require a\\nClerk session.\\n\\n## SSO is stubbed, on purpose\\n\\n`GET /auth/sso/login` and `POST /auth/sso/callback` answer `501 Not\\nImplemented` with a clear detail message. SAML requires the Clerk Enterprise\\ntier; returning an explicit 501 is more useful to integrators than a fake\\nredirect, and it documents the plan boundary in code.\\n\\n## Two case studies on curl\\n\\nWe ran the analyser over `lib/cookie.c` and `lib/http2.c` from curl. Nine\\nfindings across five CWE classes, including a potential data race and a NUL\\nbypass in cookie validation. None is a proof of concept, and the reports say so.\\nBoth are published, along with a draft disclosure note.\\n\\n## Fixed: MDX comments\\n\\nThe documentation site has been failing to build since the legal pages landed:\\nDocusaurus compiles `.md` as MDX, and MDX does not accept HTML comments. The\\nmirrors now use the MDX comment syntax. If your own site mixes HTML comments\\ninto Markdown, this is the release that would have caught it for us."},{"id":"/2026/10/18/welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/welcome","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-welcome.md","title":"Welcome to Vishap","description":"Automated security analysis for code, configuration, specs, logs and data.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"announcement","permalink":"/vishap/blog/tags/announcement"}],"readingTime":1.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Welcome to Vishap","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["announcement"],"description":"Automated security analysis for code, configuration, specs, logs and data."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Vishap v6.3.1 \u2014 Multi-tenant RLS + Audit Export","permalink":"/vishap/blog/2026/10/18/v631"}},"content":"Vishap is a SaaS platform for automated security analysis of engineering\\nmaterials \u2014 source code, configuration files, specifications, logs and structured\\ndata. You give it a file; it returns a report you can act on.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nMost security tooling forces a choice. Rule-based scanners are precise but blind\\nto anything nobody wrote a rule for. A general-purpose chat model reads broadly\\nbut produces prose you cannot file as evidence. Vishap runs both and keeps the\\nresults separate: deterministic SAST/SCA findings on one side, model reasoning on\\nthe other, synthesised into one document.\\n\\nEach report contains:\\n\\n- **Findings** mapped to CWE and OWASP Top 10, with severity and the function or\\n  file they point at.\\n- **A STRIDE threat model** derived from those findings rather than from a\\n  generic checklist.\\n- **Refactoring options** with effort estimates in days, so the report can go\\n  into planning as-is.\\n- **An audit trail** with a SHA-256 hash of the analysed slice, the provenance\\n  commit, and PII-redaction flags.\\n\\nThe report is deliberately honest about confidence. When something looks wrong\\nbut cannot be proven from the material, it says so and asks for a human review\\ninstead of asserting a vulnerability. That is why reports end in\\n`REQUIRES_REVIEW` more often than in a clean `SAFE`.\\n\\nVishap is available as a web app, a CLI, an HTTP API and a GitHub App. The\\nCLI exits non-zero when a quality gate fails, so it drops into a CI pipeline\\nwithout parsing output. The GitHub App comments on pull requests.\\n\\nStart with the [getting started guide](/docs/getting-started), or read what came\\nout when we pointed it at [curl\'s production code](/docs/case-studies/curl-cookie-c)."}]}}')}}]);